| Dependency | License | Closed-source impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| `requests==2.32` | Apache-2.0 | Fine. Include the license text and any NOTICE file in your distribution. |\n| `somelib==1.1` | GPL-3.0 | **Blocker** if you distribute it with, or linked into, your product. |\n| `left-pad==0.2` | MIT | Fine. Keep the copyright and license notice. |\n\n**Why GPL-3.0 is a problem**\n- If you ship a binary, installer, container image, or app that includes `somelib`, GPL-3.0 can require you to offer the corresponding source for the combined work under GPL terms. That is incompatible with closed source.\n- Whether your code counts as a \"combined work\" depends on how it's used (import, linking, bundling). The conservative reading, which the FSF holds, is that importing it in the same process counts.\n- GPL-3.0 also has patent-license and anti-\"tivoization\" terms. These matter if you ship on devices or hardware.\n- It matters less if the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of. GPL-3.0 has no network-use clause (AGPL does), but check this with counsel for your delivery model.\n\n**Options**\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative, or write your own.\n2. Check whether the authors sell a commercial license (common for dual-licensed libraries).\n3. Isolate it as a separate process that talks over a CLI or network boundary. This is a legal gray area, so get counsel's review first.\n4. Open-source your product under GPL-compatible terms, if that's acceptable.\n\n**Other things to check**\n- **Transitive dependencies.** `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. `somelib` and `requests` may pull in others, such as LGPL or GPL packages, so scan the full tree.\n- **Label accuracy.** I'm relying on the license labels in the file. I haven't verified them against the actual packages. Check each package's real license metadata and its repo's LICENSE file.\n- **Version pins.** `requests==2.32` is loosely pinned, so use an exact patch version. Run a vulnerability scan (CVEs) on all three before you ship.\n- **Attribution.** Add a third-party notices file to the product for the Apache and MIT components.\n\nI haven't checked how `somelib` is used in your code. If you want, I can look at that, or run a full transitive license and CVE scan. This isn't legal advice, so have counsel confirm the GPL decision before release.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}